<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/expert/behavioural-neuroscience">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Behavioural neuroscience (biological psychology) explains <strong>behaviour through its
    biological machinery</strong> — genes, neurons, hormones and circuits. It is the ground truth
    layer beneath the psychology ladder: every finding in memory, perception or motivation
    ultimately must connect to tissue, and this is the field that checks.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="hormones, reward circuits, and the causal methods" widget="equationStep" config={behavneuroCfg} />

  <h2>Findings that changed the frame</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stress is a biological loan:</strong> the HPA axis mobilises brilliantly for short threats and damages metabolism, immunity and memory for chronic ones (Sapolsky's baboon studies — stress hierarchies measured in the wild).</li>
    <li><strong>The two-axis stress response:</strong> fast (norepinephrine, seconds) and slow (cortisol, minutes–hours) — different timescales, different costs. Chronic activation is the disease mechanism.</li>
    <li><strong>Sleep is behavioural neuroscience:</strong> memory consolidation, glymphatic clearing, emotional recalibration — sleep deprivation impairs all three measurably. The cheapest intervention in the field.</li>
    <li><strong>Exercise as neuromodulator:</strong> aerobic exercise raises BDNF (brain fertiliser), improves mood and cognition with effect sizes rivalling medication for mild depression — the prescription with the best side-effect profile.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Sapolsky, Why Zebras Don't Get Ulcers; Rosenzweig et al., Biological Psychology; Carlson, Physiology of Behavior.</p>
</TopicPage>
